{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q014_paper_example_reclassification_pa1492","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts: The API's current release is 2.08 (stable label 2.08-stable; latest periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16). PDB 1t1j has domains d1t1ja_ and d1t1jb_ in SCOP 1.69, both sunid 106249 (sccs c.137.1.1, 'Hypothetical protein PA1492', Pseudomonas aeruginosa). In 1.69 the lineage is c.137 (fold, a fold of its own, sunid 110459) > c.137.1 (superfamily, sunid 110460) > c.137.1.1 (family, sunid 110461). In 2.08 (and the 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic-update selector), the same sunid 106249 is now domain d1t1ja1 (A:1-118) / d1t1jb1 (B:1-118), sccs c.23.14.2, lineage c.23 (Flavodoxin-like, sunid 52171) > c.23.14 (N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like, sunid 52309) > c.23.14.2 (family 'Hypothetical protein PA1492', sunid 110461). PDB 1f8y domains d1f8ya_ and d1f8yb_ are c.23.14.1 (Nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase, family sunid 52310, protein sunid 52311) in both 1.69 and 2.08, with the same c.23 fold and c.23.14 superfamily. The 2.08 1t1j entry also lists extra domains d1t1ja2 (l.1.1.1, A:0-0) and d1t1jb2 (l.1.1.1, B:-6-0), which I did not interpret. Neither the PA1492 domain nor the 1f8y domain has homology warnings in 2.08 or 1.69 (the 2.08 PA1492 domain has only a 'Structural genomics target' comment; 1f8y has ligand comments).

Interpretation: The change was a reassignment of PA1492 from its own fold (c.137, where it shared no superfamily with 1f8y) to the same superfamily as nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase (c.23.14). In 2.08 the two domains share superfamily c.23.14 but are in different families (c.23.14.2 vs c.23.14.1). SCOPe therefore asserts probable common ancestry at the superfamily level, and no closer family-level relationship. Nothing about this change establishes a shared substrate, reaction, or activity for PA1492; that cannot be inferred from superfamily placement.

